        "problem": "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{e}^{-x/4}}{2}?$",
        "worked_solution": "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{e}^{-x/4}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-x/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-4{e}^{-x/4}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-x/4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=0,3,6.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14041209/assets/a4b534bd33daabc94aa3c86935b1fd4c0be723fafbc418ed382716541a864293.png)",
